          "content": "Two furnaces advertise the same nominal peak temperature but use different heating ramps. A tracer is deposited on a polycrystalline alloy with non-equilibrium vacancy content from earlier processing. A colleague proposes comparing final profiles after equal clock times at peak temperature. This fictional situation will be revisited in the integrated project. Start by distinguishing direct observations from physical descriptions and from parameters that a mathematical model proposes to infer. Write a list of controlled quantities, including imposed sample temperature, the physical boundary and the observation interval. Do not assume that the smoothness of a concentration profile confirms the absence of reactions or short-circuit paths.\n\nCompute D(T) from an illustrative Arrhenius expression and integrate \u222bD[T(t)]dt numerically for each full thermal trajectory. Predict a diffusion length from each diffusion clock. Refit a straight line to synthetic ln D versus 1/T data and examine the sensitivity to a \u00b15 K temperature calibration offset. The mathematics provides a disciplined way to compare outcomes but still depends on the geometry and the meaning of the concentration variable. Across the five lessons, students should practice setting up a concrete mass or species balance, evaluating at least one numerical scale, and proposing a measurement that would discriminate among mechanisms. A complete argument identifies what a result cannot establish, even when a plotted trend agrees with an intuitive expectation.",
