          "content": "Two porous oxide ceramics contain equal chemical compositions but have different grain sizes and pore connectivity. A tracer section exhibits a long tail in one specimen. The investigator attempts to fit one uniform diffusivity to both the central profile and the tail. This fictional situation will be revisited in the integrated project. Start by distinguishing direct observations from physical descriptions and from parameters that a mathematical model proposes to infer. Write a list of controlled quantities, including imposed sample temperature, the physical boundary and the observation interval. Do not assume that the smoothness of a concentration profile confirms the absence of reactions or short-circuit paths.\n\nConstruct separate conduction analogies for connected fast paths and lattice regions. Compare the parallel-path estimate with a series-path lower-bound. Map boundary coverage as a function of depth, and show how changing geometry alone alters effective permeability even if each phase\u2019s intrinsic coefficient remains unchanged. The mathematics provides a disciplined way to compare outcomes but still depends on the geometry and the meaning of the concentration variable. Across the five lessons, students should practice setting up a concrete mass or species balance, evaluating at least one numerical scale, and proposing a measurement that would discriminate among mechanisms. A complete argument identifies what a result cannot establish, even when a plotted trend agrees with an intuitive expectation.",
